Q1. Deadlock Debugging
Condition causing deadlock:
P1 locks R1 and waits for R2.
P2 locks R2 and waits for R1.
Both wait for each other forever (Circular Wait), causing deadlock.

Optimization:
Follow a fixed resource ordering.
Example: Both processes should always lock R1 first, then R2.
This removes circular wait and prevents deadlock.

Result: Circular wait is removed, so deadlock cannot occur.

Q2. CPU Utilization Problem
Cause:
Processes repeatedly change from Ready → Waiting → Ready due to frequent I/O operations.
CPU remains idle while processes wait for I/O, resulting in low CPU utilization.

Optimization:
Increase CPU-I/O overlap using multiprogramming.
Improve scheduling (Round Robin/MLFQ) and reduce unnecessary waiting.

Result
Better CPU utilization.
Reduced idle time.
Improved throughput and response time

Q3. Memory Bottleneck Debugging
Memory usage:
Code = 5 MB
Heap = 150 MB
Stack = 25 × 1 MB = 25 MB
Total = 180 MB

Issue:
Too many threads increase stack usage and page faults, reducing performance.

Optimizations:
Reduce the number of threads or use a thread pool.
Reduce stack size or optimize memory allocation.

Result
Fewer page faults.
Faster execution.
Better memory utilization.

Q4. Starvation Debugging
Root cause:
P5 is in Q3 (lowest priority).
Higher-priority queues (Q1 and Q2) keep getting CPU time.
P5 never gets executed (Starvation).

Optimization:
Use Aging to gradually increase the priority of waiting processes.
This ensures fairness and prevents starvation.

Result
Fair CPU allocation.
No starvation.
Improved response for all processes.

Q5. Context-Switch Overhead Debugging
Why context switching is high:
Very small time quantum (1 ms) causes frequent preemption.
High I/O frequency also increases context switches.

Optimizations:
Increase the time quantum (e.g., 5–10 ms).
Reduce unnecessary preemption and improve I/O scheduling (or batch I/O operations)

Result
Lower context-switch overhead.
Better CPU performance.
Increased system throughput.
